  {
   "id": 506341,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<b>Markup.</b> In this exercise, we introduce the concept of <b>markup</b>. The <b>markup</b> on an item is the difference between how much a store sells an item for and how much the store paid for the item. Suppose Wegmans (a northeastern U.S. grocery chain) buys cereal at $1.50 per box and sells the cereal for $2.29.<br>\n<ol type=\"a\" start=\"1\">\n<li>Determine the markup in dollars.</li>\n<li>The markup is what percent of the original cost? Round the percentage to one decimal place.</li>\n</ol>",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 506342,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "In this exercise, we explore what happens when an item is marked up by a percentage, and then marked down using the same percentage.<br>\nWegmans purchases an item for $5 per unit. The markup on the item is 25%.<br>\n<ol type=\"a\" start=\"1\">\n<li>Calculate the markup on the item, in dollars.</li>\n<li>What is the price for which Wegmans sells the item? This is the price Wegmans paid, plus the markup.</li>\n<li>Suppose Wegmans then offers a 25% discount on the sale price of the item (found in part b). In dollars, how much is the discount?</li>\n<li>Determine the price of the item after the discount (this is the sales price of the item minus the discount). Round to two decimal places.</li>\n<li>Is the new price after the markup and discount equal to the price Wegmans paid for the item? Explain.</li>\n</ol>",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 506343,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<b>Repeated Discounts.</b> In this exercise, we explore applying more than one discount to an item.<br>\nSuppose a store cuts the price on an item by 50%, and then offers a coupon for 25% off any sale item. We will find the price of the item after applying the sale price and the coupon discount.<br>\n<ol type=\"a\" start=\"1\">\n<li>The original price was $150. After the 50% discount, what is the price of the item?</li>\n<li>The coupon is applied to the discount price. The coupon is for 25%. Find 25% of the sale price (found in part a).</li>\n<li>Find the price after applying the coupon (this is the value from part a minus the value from part b).</li>\n<li>The total amount saved on the item is the original price after all the discounts. Determine the total amount saved by subtracting the final price paid (part c) from the original price of the item.</li>\n<li>Determine the effective discount percentage, which is the total amount saved divided by the original price of the item.</li>\n<li>Was the effective discount percentage equal to 75%, which would be the 50% plus the 25%? Explain.</li></ol>",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  }
